index cb55706..6364aa8 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -5,11 +5,39 @@ Account password reset flow.
 ## Usage
 
 ```python
-from accounts.reset import request_reset
+from accounts.reset import complete_reset, request_reset
 
 token = request_reset("user@example.com")
+
+# ... user submits the token and a new password ...
+
+ok = complete_reset("user@example.com", token, "new-password")
 ```
 
+`request_reset` issues a token for the account and returns it. Each call adds a
+new outstanding token for that account rather than replacing the previous one,
+so more than one token can be valid for an account at the same time (for
+example, if the user asks for a new email after losing the first one).
+
+`complete_reset` verifies the token, and if it is valid:
+
+- stores `new_password` as the account's password (via `accounts.crypto`), and
+- discards every outstanding token for the account, including the one just
+  used and any others, so none of them can be used again.
+
+A token is valid only if all of the following hold:
+
+- it was issued for that account (an unknown token fails),
+- it has not already been used in a completed reset,
+- it is being used for the first time in this call (each token is single
+  use), and
+- it is being checked within 30 minutes of being issued (`TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS`
+  in `accounts.reset`); older tokens are treated as expired.
+
+`complete_reset` returns `False` for an unknown, already-used, or expired
+token rather than raising, since those are expected outcomes of user-supplied
+input, not exceptional conditions.
+
 Password storage and token comparison live in `accounts.crypto`, which the reset
-flow calls rather than hashing or comparing anything itself. Issuing a token and
-checking one are implemented; completing a reset is not.
+flow calls rather than hashing or comparing anything itself. Issuing a token,
+checking one, and completing a reset are all implemented.
